WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.892
This example uses CASE inside an aggregate.

00:00:02.892 --> 00:00:08.152
For each admission, the CASE expression asks whether the diagnosis matches a condition.

00:00:08.152 --> 00:00:13.637
A matching row contributes a value such as one, and a non-matching row contributes zero.

00:00:13.637 --> 00:00:17.392
SUM then adds those contributions inside each patient group.

00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:21.457
Run the query and inspect the separate pneumonia and fracture counts.

00:00:21.457 --> 00:00:26.132
Before running it, choose one patient from the data and predict the two counts.

00:00:26.132 --> 00:00:28.792
Then compare your prediction with the result.

00:00:28.792 --> 00:00:33.272
This pattern is flexible because CASE can handle more than simple equality.

00:00:33.272 --> 00:00:39.212
You can build conditions based on ranges, multiple columns, or more complex business rules.

00:00:39.212 --> 00:00:45.082
When using CASE for counting, make sure the values you return are appropriate for the aggregate you apply.

00:00:45.082 --> 00:00:51.827
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:51.827 --> 00:00:55.842
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:55.842 --> 00:00:58.382
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:58.382 --> 00:01:03.617
In a real setting such as a student-success report comparing course-level outcomes,

00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:09.762
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:09.762 --> 00:01:12.017
before discussing syntax.

00:01:12.017 --> 00:01:18.060
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
